#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb7675da-3715-50c7-8e7e-0edb9d8056e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\cot(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb7675da-3715-50c7-8e7e-0edb9d8056e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\cot(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289956} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6a8c2fc-1baf-5461-8089-f41525dae5c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d598999c-b003-52da-8100-10acae05a1ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-351724" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)={csc}^{2}⁡(t),$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=0$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+C \\\\ 0 & =-1+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43e68991-37f0-543e-a4da-b78b395a8494" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Acceleration, Initial Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2dcebdf-97d5-57dd-b942-fa611dad85be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\frac{1}{2}e^{2t} + te^{2} + e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063742} {:answer/id #uuid "cb934697-87e2-5dfc-b265-22e02c28d7ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\frac{1}{2}e^{2t} + \\frac{1}{2}e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063743} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e571db-224b-531a-a3b3-d2057cb71d42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 2e^{2t} + e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063744} {:answer/id #uuid "f5175e4b-5c73-5462-9a6c-4af62396132a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = e^{2t} + te^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063745} {:answer/id #uuid "6203d699-025c-51df-a2ae-aca97eb36849" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 2e^{2t} + te^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb934697-87e2-5dfc-b265-22e02c28d7ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\frac{1}{2}e^{2t} + \\frac{1}{2}e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063743} :answer-field/id #uuid "9aa5641a-c627-5f3c-a1ef-ba6f561c5bc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063741}] :question/id #uuid "8c0db8b8-4135-59b8-8dbc-bc9a7fe69ee1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81439" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t) = 2e^{2t}$, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x = e^{2}$ and velocity $v = e^{2}$ when $t = 1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t > 0$." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43e68991-37f0-543e-a4da-b78b395a8494" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Acceleration, Initial Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd91ab91-5062-5dbc-8ee3-cbcbdc22bbbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2t+3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290001} {:answer/id #uuid "0f998b3e-512e-5a38-9369-447e501d61bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290002} {:answer/id #uuid "09154eac-a091-51cd-9bcd-01505287cd20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290003} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc75397-04fe-5142-95a6-3d79a59998ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290004} {:answer/id #uuid "a6831d72-7e48-59cf-9c98-9602655059df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09154eac-a091-51cd-9bcd-01505287cd20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290003} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f49fdf-2810-54d5-9246-8a3b730d3fdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "641e7b63-702c-540a-b9fd-6b417e1a8c11" :question/math-academy-id "q-84244" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=2\\pi $ and velocity $v=4$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ 4 & =2(1)+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2\\pi .$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+K \\\\ 2\\pi  & =-2(0)+\\pi +K \\\\ K & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi .\n$$"}]}
